WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.030
So it's very important to feel
that whatever you're doing,

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:04.620
that's what you want to be doing.

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:08.850
Hi, Steve Kaufmann here and today,
I want to talk about burnout.

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:13.140
Something that we all experience at
different times in learning languages.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.890
Remember, if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:18.390
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:22.050
And if you follow me on a
podcast, please leave a review.

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:27.950
So to my mind, burnout is
associated with a number of things.

00:00:28.510 --> 00:00:33.585
First of all with what I call
the doldrums, the plateau, when

00:00:33.585 --> 00:00:36.075
you hit that point where you
feel you're not making progress.

00:00:36.644 --> 00:00:38.295
So then we are discouraged.

00:00:38.835 --> 00:00:44.625
You know, I've said before at the
early stage in learning a language, we

00:00:44.655 --> 00:00:46.575
actually make quite a bit of progress.

00:00:46.575 --> 00:00:48.855
It's a steep climb, but
we're actually climbing.

00:00:48.855 --> 00:00:49.995
We can see where we were.

00:00:50.025 --> 00:00:52.485
We look down, we see where
we were a month earlier.

00:00:52.485 --> 00:00:53.265
We knew nothing.

00:00:53.415 --> 00:00:55.665
Now we can say something,
we understand something.

00:00:55.995 --> 00:01:00.660
So that's the sort of early call
it three to six months, the early

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:02.879
period in learning a new language.

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:08.250
But then we sort of come into a stage
where the words don't appear so often.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:11.220
Uh, we've put in a lot of time,
we think we've put in a lot of

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:14.039
time and yet we have that feeling
that we're not getting any better.

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:17.190
Part of the problem is
we're too hard on ourselves.

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:21.930
And I think part of the problem, why
people experience burnout is they have

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:26.009
unrealistic expectations about how
quickly they can learn a language.

00:01:26.774 --> 00:01:32.445
Manfred Spitzer, whom I often quote,
a German neuro scientist, said the

00:01:32.445 --> 00:01:35.115
brain cannot do otherwise than learn.

00:01:35.175 --> 00:01:35.955
The brain learns.

00:01:36.134 --> 00:01:41.774
The brain is exposed to stimulus, stimuli,
and it forms patterns to deal with the

00:01:41.774 --> 00:01:47.115
world that the brain is sort of confronted
with experiencing, but so he says the

00:01:47.115 --> 00:01:48.704
brain cannot do otherwise than learn.

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:51.480
However, the brain learns slowly.

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:57.030
So we have to accept that as long
as we are exposing ourselves to

00:01:57.030 --> 00:02:02.610
the language, reading, listening,
speaking, we are acquiring the

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:04.590
language, but we're doing it slowly.

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:09.030
And I think a lot of frustration
comes from unrealistic expectations.

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:13.470
Learning it slowly means it, the
language will continue to remain fuzzy.

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:18.105
Even something you listened to initially
three months ago and you're back, go

00:02:18.105 --> 00:02:21.345
back to it now and you say, I should be
able to understand it very easily and

00:02:21.345 --> 00:02:24.885
you go there and you can't understand it
easily, or you feel you should be able

00:02:24.885 --> 00:02:26.714
to express yourself better and you can't.

00:02:27.105 --> 00:02:31.125
So I think this, this burnout comes
from the feeling that you're doing

00:02:31.125 --> 00:02:34.635
the same thing over and over again and
not achieving, achieving any results.

00:02:34.785 --> 00:02:38.475
So I think the first thing you have
to do is to say let's be realistic

00:02:38.505 --> 00:02:42.725
in what I'm going to achieve, and
let's try to enjoy what I'm doing.

00:02:43.334 --> 00:02:45.234
Let's make sure that while you...

00:02:45.234 --> 00:02:47.015
so to enjoy what you're doing A.

00:02:47.015 --> 00:02:48.855
You gotta look for content
that's of interest.

00:02:48.855 --> 00:02:52.935
So I put a lot of effort in my
language learning into looking for

00:02:52.935 --> 00:02:54.345
things that are of interest to me.

00:02:54.435 --> 00:02:59.084
So even if I feel that I'm not improving,
at least I'm not just going over the

00:02:59.084 --> 00:03:00.375
same material over and over again.

00:03:00.614 --> 00:03:04.325
At least I'm learning something
new or, you know, I, I found this,

00:03:04.375 --> 00:03:08.565
these, uh, Jordanian cartoons that
are funny and, and, and give a bit of

00:03:08.565 --> 00:03:11.024
a glimpse of Jordanian family life.

00:03:11.505 --> 00:03:15.855
Uh, and I'll leave a link to them here,
but I'm finding it's it's enjoyable.

00:03:15.885 --> 00:03:20.445
So whether I'm improving or not, I'm not
really sure but I am enjoying, you know,

00:03:20.445 --> 00:03:22.095
reading those and listening to them.

00:03:22.364 --> 00:03:27.674
So, uh, don't put unrealistic
expectations on yourself, find

00:03:27.674 --> 00:03:29.984
ways to enjoy the process.

00:03:30.285 --> 00:03:35.174
And also, and this is I think, a little
difficult today, you know, in the old days

00:03:35.174 --> 00:03:36.685
we got a textbook and that's what we had.

00:03:36.954 --> 00:03:38.174
And that's the only thing we used.

00:03:38.355 --> 00:03:40.334
Now there's lots of options out there.

00:03:40.545 --> 00:03:43.575
So it's very important to feel
that whatever you're doing,

00:03:43.605 --> 00:03:45.125
that's what you want to be doing.

00:03:45.725 --> 00:03:49.380
Don't be thinking about other things that
you would like to do, make sure that you

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:54.120
don't begrudge yourself the time you're
putting into the language, that's going

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:56.190
to make your attitude more positive.

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:57.930
So I enjoy what I'm doing.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:03:59.520
I'm glad I'm doing it.

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:03.330
I may not really feel that
I'm progressing, but I have to

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:06.960
trust that my brain is gradually
getting used to the language.

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:09.300
I can assure you it's a long process.

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:13.350
Now, another thing that I think
is very important in order not

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:16.290
to get burned out is variety.

00:04:16.590 --> 00:04:18.660
So variety comes in different...

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:19.830
in a variety of ways.

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:22.890
So the variety can be the
content you're listening to.

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:28.380
Vary it between advanced and beginner and
different subjects and things of interest.

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:33.840
Um, I find myself, for example, I say
I should do Persian cause I have a

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:37.050
tutor tonight, but I'm actually more
interested in doing the Arabic tonight.

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:40.290
Well, then do what you want to
do, make sure you're having fun.

00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:42.270
So, but there's the variety there.

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:46.740
Um, again, the variety in terms
of the kinds of activities.

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:48.630
You can read, you can listen.

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:52.740
Uh, I'm also gonna do another
video where I talk about shadowing.

00:04:53.130 --> 00:04:54.300
Uh, flashcards.

00:04:54.510 --> 00:04:58.740
Variety is going to make you feel
less frustrated so you can do

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:02.190
one type of activity for a while
before you get totally tired of

00:05:02.190 --> 00:05:04.110
it, go off and do something else.

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:08.340
And finally, if you're really
burned out, take some time off.

00:05:09.390 --> 00:05:12.760
One of the interesting things about
language learning is when you back away

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:17.955
from it a lot of what you've been learning
continues to gestate in your brain.

00:05:18.255 --> 00:05:20.715
I've called it this
period of benign neglect.

00:05:21.015 --> 00:05:25.095
So don't feel that if you sort of stop
studying for a few weeks, you're going

00:05:25.095 --> 00:05:26.295
to lose everything you've learned.

00:05:26.385 --> 00:05:26.985
You won't.

00:05:27.075 --> 00:05:30.795
In fact, when you pick it up again
very quickly you'll get back to where

00:05:30.795 --> 00:05:34.125
you were and then you'll find that
you'll improve more quickly again.

00:05:34.335 --> 00:05:38.895
So those are some of the strategies that I
would recommend in order to avoid burnout.

00:05:39.495 --> 00:05:40.275
Thank you for listening.

